WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:04.012
Reducing extraneous load means removing presentation friction that does not support learning.

00:00:04.012 --> 00:00:07.272
Cluttered slides, decorative graphics, unnecessary detail, weak examples, unclear labels,

00:00:07.272 --> 00:00:09.228
and poor pacing all consume attention.

00:00:09.228 --> 00:00:10.858
Learners may still work hard,

00:00:10.858 --> 00:00:15.749
but their effort goes into sorting through the material rather than understanding the core relationship.

00:00:15.749 --> 00:00:18.683
Clean design is not about making a course plain.

00:00:18.683 --> 00:00:21.291
It is about making the important structure visible.

00:00:21.291 --> 00:00:24.225
Split attention is a common source of extraneous load.

00:00:24.225 --> 00:00:28.463
It happens when learners must divide attention between poorly integrated sources of information,

00:00:28.463 --> 00:00:33.354
such as a diagram on one part of the page and the explanation somewhere else.

00:00:33.354 --> 00:00:37.592
The learner has to search, match, and remember pieces before understanding can begin.

00:00:37.592 --> 00:00:40.526
Integrating labels, placing explanations close to the relevant visual,

00:00:40.526 --> 00:00:43.786
and aligning narration with the diagram can reduce that burden.

00:00:43.786 --> 00:00:45.416
Redundancy can also create load.

00:00:45.416 --> 00:00:48.676
Repeating the same information in unnecessary forms may seem helpful,

00:00:48.676 --> 00:00:51.284
but it can force learners to process clutter.

00:00:51.284 --> 00:00:51.936
For example,

00:00:51.936 --> 00:00:55.849
reading dense on-screen text word for word while the learner sees the

00:00:55.849 --> 00:00:57.479
same text can become inefficient.

00:00:57.479 --> 00:01:00.413
The issue is not that repetition is always bad.

00:01:00.413 --> 00:01:04.651
The issue is whether each representation adds value or merely competes for attention.

00:01:04.651 --> 00:01:06.607
Navigation and pacing matter as well.

00:01:06.607 --> 00:01:10.519
If learners are uncertain where to click, what section they are in,

00:01:10.519 --> 00:01:13.127
or how examples relate to the current concept,

00:01:13.127 --> 00:01:16.714
working memory is spent on the interface instead of the idea.

00:01:16.714 --> 00:01:20.952
Strong materials help learners focus on the essential relationship: what is being explained,

00:01:20.952 --> 00:01:23.234
why it matters, how the parts connect,

00:01:23.234 --> 00:01:26.820
and what the learner should be able to do with it.
